=== 2. Simulated non-invariance planted on item 5 (Appetite change) ===
Group B's thresholds on item 5 are shifted on the latent response scale.
Everything else is invariant by construction, n = 2,500 per group.
The criterion here is the chi-square rise and the score-test ranking, not
delta CFI: data generated from a true one-factor model fits so well that
CFI is 1.000 at every rung, so delta CFI has no room to move. The 0.010
cutoff is a rule for approximate misfit in real data, not for a model that
is correct by construction. This is also the check that caught the
intercept-identification bug documented in R/invariance.R.

shift +0.0: chisq rise    11.3 on 18 df,  worst item = 4 Low energy            planted item rank 6/9  X2    1.8 vs   3.7 next
shift +0.2: chisq rise    33.9 on 18 df,  worst item = 5 Appetite change       planted item rank 1/9  X2   18.6 vs   4.9 next
shift +0.4: chisq rise    45.6 on 18 df,  worst item = 5 Appetite change       planted item rank 1/9  X2   47.8 vs   7.4 next
shift +0.6: chisq rise   205.8 on 18 df,  worst item = 5 Appetite change       planted item rank 1/9  X2  225.8 vs   9.1 next

With no shift the chi-square rise is at its degrees of freedom and the
item ranking is noise, as it must be. With a shift the ranking puts the
planted item first by a wide margin, so the localisation step points at the
right item rather than scattering blame across the questionnaire.

=== 3. Recovery of a known gap decomposition ===
Simulate a true latent mean difference of 0.25 SD plus a planted
intercept shift on item 5, then see whether decompose_gap splits the
observed sum-score gap the way it was built. 20 replications, because one
replication cannot distinguish a biased estimator from sampling noise.

true latent gap        +0.250 SD   recovered mean +0.242 SD  (sd 0.039)
true measurement part  +0.300 pts  recovered mean +0.297 pts (sd 0.025)
true latent part       +1.583 pts  recovered mean +1.531 pts (sd 0.243)
mean observed gap +1.831 pts, mean model-implied +1.828 pts
bias in the measurement share: true 15.9%, recovered 16.5%

